Z||000003||KRMFiero||01-04-2001||08:10 PM||||I looked at the door, doesnt look like it will be easy to pull this thing apart,   any tips for doing this so my door looks like a 2 year  old didnt pull it apart heheh<P><BR>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroJoe||01-04-2001||08:51 PM||||First off, if you dont already have a door panel remover tool, go to autozone and get one.  It looks like a flat fork, it is used to pry the "christmas tree" fasteners out of the door.  A very worthwhile tool.  Anyway, if you have power windows, it will be easier with than manual as you dont have the crank handle to worry about.  I've only taken off door panels with power windows.<P>Anyway, if my memory serves correctly:<P>Take off armrest, screw holes are visible.<BR>Next for the black lock trim piece.  Using a small screwdriver and a lot of care, take off the little pegasus emblem, and remove the screw within.  Next, there is a round plug covering another screw behind the door release handle.  Remove the plug, and then the screw.  You will then have to pop off the door lock plunger.  It is snapped to the door lock linkage, use a screwdriver to pry it off.  Now you will have to work the trim piece out and around the door release handle.  Get this done, and if you have power locks, disconnect the switch wiring.<BR>Using the door panel removal tool, pry up on the side of the door panel, between the door and the panel.  You should be able to feel where the fasteners are, so put the one side of the "fork" on each side of the fastener, and pry up to pop it off.  Go around all 3 sides of the door until they are all off.  All you will have to do now is pull up and away, the door panel "hangs" by the top part of the door.<P>Hope this helps, and correct me if I'm wrong with any of it.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||01-04-2001||09:41 PM||||Michigan Fiero Club website has a write-up about installing power windows, but in the write up it details how to get in the door panel. You will have to drill out the 4 rivits holding the regulator in. And to remount it use self threading 5/16(???)bolts. The regulator has two arms with wheels at each arms end, 4 wheels total. I suspect an arm rusted and broke, or a wheel fell off the track. <BR>Be carefull working in the steel door panel. I cut myself quite badly trying to remove one of the regulators in mine, and still have scars(plural)to prove it. You will probably have to remove the regulator to get a good look in door panel.||||reg||1
